<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Managing Stress in Changing Times</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Forest Bathing and Other Biohacks</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reading: Analyze authentic non-fiction texts. Reading: Understand the relationship between main points and examples in various texts.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Reading: Can compare examples from two wellness articles, evaluating how different stress-management techniques impact mental health (e.g., "Guided meditation may work better for beginners, while mindfulness is effective for daily stress relief.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can understand the connections between the main points of an article on stress-relief methods and examples of their impact on mental health (e.g., "Deep breathing exercises calm the body and mind, reducing anxiety.").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can recognize examples that illustrate wellness practices in informational texts, using these to support personal wellness plans (e.g., "Listening to calming music can help me focus during study sessions.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a vivid image of a lush forest or a person practicing deep breathing. Students are asked to brainstorm words related to "stress relief" or "relaxation" that they remember from the previous lesson. The teacher writes their ideas on the board, including words like <em>mindfulness, meditation, nature, breathing, exercise, focus, calm, soothe</em>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces the lesson's reading focus by stating, "Today, we are going to read two articles about effective wellness techniques to manage stress. We will learn how to identify main ideas and supporting examples." The teacher models skimming a short text excerpt (e.g., a headline and first paragraph) and identifying key terms. The teacher also models the pronunciation of multisyllabic words like "mindfulness" (/ˈmaɪnd.fəl.nəs/) and "beneficial" (/ˌben.ɪˈfɪʃ.əl/), emphasizing the alternation of full and reduced vowels.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is our main skill focus for today? Is it speaking or reading?" (Expected answer: "Reading.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Will we be looking for general ideas or specific details in the texts?" (Expected answer: "Both, main ideas and examples.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Authentic non-fiction article excerpts titled "Forest Bathing: Nature's Biohack for Stress Relief" (Article 1) and "Mindfulness and Deep Breathing for Daily Calm" (Article 2). The texts describe the techniques, their benefits, and include target vocabulary such as <em>forest bathing, biohack, mindfulness, meditation, deep breathing, relaxation, wellness, nature, focus, calm, reduce, improve, beneficial, soothing</em>. They also feature gerunds ("Practicing mindfulness...", "Spending time in nature..."), modals for advice ("You should try...", "It could help..."), and present continuous for trends ("People are increasingly seeking...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understands the main points and specific details of authentic non-fiction texts on familiar concrete topics related to wellness and stress management.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Reading:</b> Students read both articles quickly (1-2 minutes per article) and answer: "What is the main topic of each article?" (e.g., Article 1: Forest bathing as a biohack. Article 2: Mindfulness and deep breathing for daily calm.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students read the articles again and determine if the following statements are true or false, correcting the false ones.
                        <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Forest bathing is a technique that involves showering in the woods." (False, it's about connecting with nature.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Practicing mindfulness helps to improve focus." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Deep breathing exercises can reduce anxiety." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"People are increasingly using technology to manage stress." (False, the articles promote natural or internal methods).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Identifying main ideas and supporting examples in non-fiction texts; using gerunds, modals for advice, and present continuous; understanding and applying vocabulary related to stress management and wellness (e.g., <em>stress, wellness, relaxation, mindful, beneficial, soothing, techniques, practices, improve, reduce</em>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Main Idea & Example Match:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with main ideas from the articles (e.g., "Forest bathing promotes mental wellness.") and a list of specific examples (e.g., "reduces cortisol levels," "improves mood"). Students read the articles again, underline the main ideas in one color and the corresponding examples in another, then match them on the worksheet. This activity also reinforces the pronunciation of words like "soothing" /suːðɪŋ/ and "smooth" /smuːð/ if encountered in text examples.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Sentence Completion (Gerunds & Modals):</b> Students complete sentences based on the articles, using gerunds or modals for advice.
                        <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"__________ (practice) mindfulness is an effective way to stay calm."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You __________ (try) deep breathing exercises if you feel overwhelmed."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"__________ (spend) time in nature can truly promote relaxation."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"People __________ (seek) natural remedies for stress these days."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Peer Check & Discussion:</b> In pairs, students compare their answers from activity 1 and 2. They discuss any discrepancies, referring back to the articles to justify their choices. They also share personal connections, e.g., "Do you think forest bathing is beneficial? Why or why not?" or "What techniques should someone try to relieve stress?" (Sociolinguistic: Offering suggestions politely, using a respectful tone).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will compare and contrast the two wellness articles, evaluating how different stress-management techniques impact mental health, and use examples from the texts to support their claims. The task is structured to enhance their reading comprehension and ability to synthesize information.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading Comparison & Summary.</p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in groups of three. Each group receives a Venn diagram or a comparison chart to fill out.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They re-read "Forest Bathing: Nature's Biohack for Stress Relief" and "Mindfulness and Deep Breathing for Daily Calm."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The groups identify the main points and specific examples of how each technique (forest bathing, mindfulness, deep breathing) helps manage stress and improve mental health. They then record these in their chart, noting similarities and differences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For example, students might write: "Guided meditation may work better for beginners, while mindfulness is effective for daily stress relief," or "While forest bathing connects you with outdoors, practicing deep breathing is beneficial anywhere."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, each group prepares a short oral summary (30-60 seconds) to present their findings to the class, using target vocabulary (e.g., <em>relaxation, focus, relief, calm, promote, reduce</em>) and grammar (e.g., "<em>Practicing mindfulness</em> offers immediate <em>stress relief</em>," "You <em>should try forest bathing</em> if you love nature"). (Pragmatic: Sharing relaxation techniques, giving advice).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and presentation, focusing on the ability to identify, compare, and synthesize information from the texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their completed comparison chart/Venn diagram and participate in the group summary presentation. The teacher listens for their ability to accurately extract information and formulate comparisons from the articles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify main points and supporting examples from both texts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear comparison of stress-management techniques and their impact on mental health.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., <em>mindful, beneficial, serene, techniques</em>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammatical accuracy (e.g., correct use of gerunds, modals for advice, present continuous).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and respectful interaction within the group.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new stress-management technique I learned about today is __________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"After reading, I think listening to calming music can help me focus during study sessions because __________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel in reading and understanding the main points and examples in non-fiction articles about wellness?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one relaxation technique from the articles that you might consider trying, and why?" (e.g., "In my experience, exercise helps with focus, so I might try forest bathing to connect with nature.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The lesson successfully engaged students in analyzing authentic texts. The comparison chart activity allowed for deep processing of information and application of reading skills. Students were able to use the target vocabulary and grammar to articulate their findings. One adjustment for next time could be to provide an additional short, contrasting text on a slightly different stress-relief method to challenge students further in their comparison skills. Providing more explicit modelling of summarizing main points before the production phase might also be beneficial.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson built a strong foundation in understanding various stress-management techniques through reading. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will focus on actively discussing these techniques and sharing personal experiences with stress management, building on the content and vocabulary from today's reading activity.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>